frame.initial_lean:
My lean is Do Nothing on every field again: fps hold (code null, fps_site new_site), rate_737 hold (the only value on offer) and re787 hold.

The fps waits for a business case. Every launch is at least $4.77B below Do Nothing in every column and fails Malave's buffer on S4. Waiting has not helped: the best fps was -4.41 in 2030 and is -4.77 now. Company doctrine's row 1 points to an fps this round, but at $4.77B or more it breaks the $2B cap in H8, and Airbus's timeline does not set our date [B-2313]. When the fps does pass, I expect to bring:
- the 10-year Solo, probably at Renton under Pope's wind-down condition;
- code 7;
- no date before launch, and a conservative EIS at launch.

The 787 Re-engine is the real question, and I want the ExCo to see it plainly. On my own tests it passes:
- it is a derivative, not a clean sheet;
- the engine path is now on the record;
- there is no overlap with any fps;
- every debt line holds;
- it is +2.01 in four of five columns.
It is the kind of Re-engine my record accepts once the developments are done [B-2326]. What holds it is the Board's $1B downside limit: -1.63 in S3, which the Board has said it would veto, and against which I have no override. I will not bring the Board a case that fails its own stated test.

So I lean to hold it. I would log the $2.01B given up in S1 as the Board's risk limit, not a management premium, and say on the record that a hold now probably gives the Re-engine up for good. If either of you thinks we should take it to the Board anyway, make that case with numbers. If Pope finds a readiness gap, the hold also stands on my own grounds.

Expected column: S1, rivals hold (Low confidence on Airbus). Management premium: $0. It's so much more important that we do this right than fast, but this is not a month's wait: it is a decade, and I want that on the record too.

tests.numbers:
Ask (3).
Board booking for a 2035 launch:
- Re-engine $5B in 2040;
- 7-year fps $65.00B in 2042;
- 10-year $60.32B in 2045;
- via Embraer $100B in 2045.
No two land in the same year in any H4-legal combination.
Tracker spend (programme cash enters free cash flow at 79%):
- Re-engine: about $1.0B a year over 2035-40, about $0.8B after the 79%. Peak $0.0B.
- 10-year fps: $3.0B in 2035 (5%), peaking at $8.4B in 2041 (14%), which is $6.7B after the 79%. That is below stress FCF of $9.2B, so the peak is $0.0B.
- Strain with the Re-engine: 2038-40, $1.2B undiscounted.
Rows that break the stress line or peak above $0:
- 7-year at new sites: breaks it in every column ($0.5B; $0.7B in S5).
- 7-year with the Re-engine at new sites: breaks it in every column ($2.2-2.7B).
- 7-year at Renton with the Re-engine: one correction to your reading. It breaks the scored line only in S3 ($1.7B) and S5 ($1.9B). In S1, S2 and S4 it meets the scored line but peaks at $1.7B, $1.5B and $1.7B between checkpoints.
- Via Embraer: breaks it in every column ($1.8-3.1B alone; $0.8-5.2B with the Re-engine), except S2 with the Re-engine, which meets the line but peaks at $0.8B.
One addition to your clean list: the 7-year at Renton alone also peaks at $0.0B. The clean rows are every fps-hold row, every 10-year row and the 7-year at Renton alone.
Objective metrics for the row I recommend (fps hold + 787 Re-engine; identical for Do Nothing):
- net debt at the historical level in 2029, held at end-2035, 2040, 2045 and 2050 at $0.0B;
- stress case back to $0 in 2030 and held, peak $0.0B;
- 1 engine source (CFM) on our narrowbody in 2040-2050, and 1 even if P&W and Rolls-Royce launch;
- two makers in the market (CFM, P&W);
- share objectives missed (worst preference year 26.0% in 2050).

No live supply problem in the brief. 787 Re-engine: the GEnx upgrade launched in 2030 and has been ready since 2033 (bulletin). The engine is on the record, 7 years ahead of a 2040 EIS. Doctrine's Re-engine engine is a GEnx derivative that needs no commitment (profile §6, §11.1). 737 MAX: LEAP-1B, one source. CFM narrowbody engines, on capacity: about 2,592 a year in 2035 (684 x 2 plus 60% of Airbus's 1,020 x 2). In 2044 it is 2,685-2,948 against Airbus's 1,317, depending on the A320neo/NGSA mix (inference). We are 46-53% of CFM's narrowbody OE output. Airbus has public, rate-gated LEAP-1A and GTF agreements to about 2045 and NGSA frameworks with all three makers. Our answer at no grid cost:
- long-range, rate-gated LEAP-1B OE and spares cover through the MAX phase-out (2044-60);
- joint capacity planning with CFM, so it can serve our rate and Airbus's A320neo and NGSA [BX-1264, BX-1325];
- embedded staff on durability [B-0073, B-2097];
- LEAP durability hardware in new engines and at shop visits (CFM's stated plan);
- supplier buffers [B-2043] and fragile suppliers kept funded [B-2330];
- the public multi-source requirement.
None of it adds a second MAX source: the enabler stays unmet under Do Nothing (1 source, 2 makers).

