would_veto:
Any item with any of the following still in force:
- a binding ExCo veto, from Ghai on volumes, price/cost or premium, or from Ali on dates;
- a safety or durability flag from Ali that the CEO has not resolved cross-functionally on evidence;
- a 20% fuel-burn red line neither struck nor rebutted;
- an override we cannot verify by re-running the test on Culp's evidence.

recommendations.text:
Safety, quality and durability first. Before any launch reaches us, Ali should give us four things:
- the LEAP-1B durability kit outcome, still open from round 1;
- a test basis, not just the board date, for a Ducted ready in 2041;
- confirmation that its hot-and-harsh durability is gated by test and never traded for the date;
- any finding sent to a cross-functional team first.
If a Ducted is launched, `rationale` should rebut the 20% red line: a defensive derivative with less than half the open-fan gain, with RISE as the next engine.

Public statement:
- name no rival, GTF2 included, and blame no one;
- give no Ducted ready date before 2041;
- give no Open Fan or RISE date, and no entry before 2045;
- claim no CFM engine on an Embraer jet before 2042;
- no CFM price talk without Safran, and nothing on US-Brazil trade;
- if a Ducted is launched, call it a derivative that defends our customers' choices, never 'the next generation';
- keep the line 'CFM will commit a new engine with a committed airframer'.

board_response.response:
Taken up. The statement says:
- launched 2030, ready 2036, six years from launch;
- not yet proven, and proven before service;
- 'named alongside CFM and Rolls-Royce' (code 7, on the record).
It gives no Airbus EIS or rate, no Boeing or Embraer selection, no Joint Venture and no time-on-wing figure. Durability is phrased as a commitment. Mitchill and Eddy both cleared the draft.

tests.numbers:
The envelope passes, so ground 1 is not reached on any row. GTF2's remaining $0.33B comes out of what is left after the dividend and debt paydown, so it competes with buybacks, not the dividend. The dividend and the pre-ASR debt path are untouched, and the one-time stretch stays unused.

The 2035 position for the Finance Committee: my files hold no 2035 figure, and I will not make one up. The last anchor is 2030E on the broker models:
- net debt $16.6-21.0B (0.76-0.94x EBITDA), against $37.2-38.7B in 2023A after the ASR;
- free cash flow $12.1-12.6B;
- dividends $5.0-5.6B and buybacks $4-5B.

Management's own frame: conversion of 90-100% of adjusted net income, a dividend that grows with earnings, debt back to pre-ASR levels before buybacks, and about $6B a year of self-funded R&D and capex. Management's 2035 plan goes to the Finance Committee as a dated follow-up at the first annual GTF2 NPV review, not as a number today.

